On Sun, Aug 25, 2019 at 8:52 PM Iñaki Ucar <iu...@fedoraproject.org> wrote:
> You may want to try the future.apply package [1]. Its future_lapply
> function is a drop-in replacement for lapply and mclapply, and you
> just need to set the plan to multiprocess: if multicore is available
> (Linux and Mac), it runs in multicore mode; if not (Windows), it runs
> in multiprocess mode (just what you are doing with doParallel, but the
> easy way).

Just back at work after a holiday and tried to drop future_lapply()
into the WSL version of my code under Windows - unfortunately it
doesn't work as a true drop-in replacement for mclapply() as the
environment isn't all copied across (eg the calendars for bizdays) so
it needs the same reinitialisation as with doParallel (I presume it
would work fine after that). It's all a bit of a pain on Windows
without using WSL! Thanks for the suggestion though - syntax is
definitely easier.

tom

